281132 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186279807 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281132 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "aec2e96d-de39-5f66-aab1-77904959b9e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279403 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186279466 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280303 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186280434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186280152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281236 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e58ed6b3-82ba-5b40-8a75-2b63a00c7ed1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186280890 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1069 "q-162223" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/52/522a51527563645261a3b4c5a1472510bcb3e890fc82d3b4c1b2ccdae59b9dbd.png)\n\nConsider the affine transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \nWhat would be the image of the rectangle shown above under the action of $T?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ef668553-5d10-5944-a4d2-e24693f30a54" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186281418 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that for any affine transformation \n\n$$\nT:u⟶Au+b,\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix and $u$ and $b$ are $2\\times 1$ column vectors, we have one of the following three possibilities:\n\n- If $T$ is non-singular, i.e., when $det(A)\\ne 0,$ then the image of a two-dimensional object is a two-dimensional object. In particular, the image of a polygon with $n$ vertices is a polygon with $n$ vertices.\n\n- If $T$ is singular, i.e., when $det(A)=0,$ we have two additional subcases:\n\n- If $A$ has at least one nonzero entry, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a one-dimensional line segment.\n\n- If $A$ is the zero matrix, then the image of a two-dimensional object is a point on the plane.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our transformation.\n\nNotice that the matrix representation of the linear transformation associated with $T$ is \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nComputing the determinant of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(T) & =|\\begin{aligned}9 & -3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9\\cdot (-1)-3\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a singular nonzero matrix. \n\nTherefore, $T$ is non-invertible and the image of the rectangle under the action of $T$ is a line segment." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285204 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285204 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285204 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e8bb8d9-a3bb-5f41-8ede-1ac64d1b2be4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285298 #edb/ref 1080 "$C$ only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285298 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285298 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "36a60e37-2471-5aa2-b57a-1481a0f6284d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285337 #edb/ref 1080 "22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285337 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285337 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a58f260-85bb-5900-91d0-ca3e5d9f962c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285667 #edb/ref 1080 "15" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "5fdec9f3-318f-51f1-85f9-84a5f248750f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186285760 #edb/r